  "rationale": "Minimal-medium (DMEM 10 mM glucose/1 mM glutamine) sub-series of the HAP1 genetic-interaction study. The deposited matrix has one raw-count column per sequencing library and no gene column; prepare.R adds Gene (REAGENT_ID prefix), writes blank cells (no reads; the matrix holds no zeros) as 0, and keeps the screens used, summing each screen's endpoint libraries (A/B/C, parallel passages of one infection) into one column <GIN>_end so that the unit of replication is the screen (infection) rather than parallel passages. Library-to-screen mapping (T0 and endpoints A/B/C per GIN screen) comes from the paper's Data S1 and agrees with the GSM titles. Query: PTAR1, the paper's showcase query, screened in minimal medium as GIN043 (14-bp deletion), GIN053 (14-bp deletion) and GIN109 (independent 1-bp insertion allele); GIN108 failed the paper's QC and is excluded. Reference: QC-passing WT/parental minimal-medium screens with all libraries deposited; excluded are the diploid WT clones (GIN035, GIN054), the Houry-lab WT line (GIN175), GIN145 (endpoints not deposited) and GIN030 (its T0 library is titled GIN028_T0 on GEO, contradicting Data S1); GIN089 and GIN181 have two endpoints. Model ~ screen + phase + gi: screen absorbs each infection's T0 representation, phase (T0, end) is the common dropout, and gi marks the PTAR1 endpoints, so giPTAR1_end is the PTAR1-specific change in guide abundance beyond the WT dropout (a differential log fold change, the quantity qGI scores; negative = negative genetic interaction). With one T0 and one summed endpoint per screen, the residual dispersion is the between-screen variation of the dropout across the WT screens, and the PTAR1 effect is estimated across three independent PTAR1 infections. Screens ran to different endpoints; the qGI method adjusts for this with an M-A loess normalization that BARCS does not apply. Controls: 142 EGFP/LacZ/luciferase guides.",
  "counts_md5": "63c9ff8eeb15b7a2e755b3bfec488666"
}